# encoder_intra_dir_i16_2026-08-03 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_intra_dir_i16_2026-08-03.md and its five data files.
#
# This is a RE-MEASUREMENT of a ranked profile row, followed by a CHANGE and its
# before/after band. Lever 4 of benchmarks/encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02.md
# ("bd8 lowbd intra predictors", +14.54 ms / 12.3 % of the gap PROJECTED). That
# ceiling is arithmetic on a measured self-cost taken at a baseline three
# landings ago; this file is what the row is worth now and what the change
# actually returned.

date                 2026-08-03
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin arm64)
baseline commit      0279544522a77a7d6be81a66b02a53a1abdabed8 (main at session start)
landing commit       71c924a72a4221a42aee903386b6b5388a8c1fba
worktree             zenav1-aom--intra8, detached (removed at the end of the session)
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f (v3.14.1)
rustc                stable toolchain; no -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch, what users get)
threading            single-threaded on every arm
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`
box load             1.3-2.7 of 12 cores (`uptime` before each band; recorded in the .md)

## Profile cell — unchanged from all four prior encoder studies, for continuity

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv (the file the
2026-08-01 cross-encoder study, both encoder profiles, the allocation landing
and the i16 forward-transform landing used). Every arm emits the same 4472-byte
stream — verified by SHA, not by byte count alone.

## Part 1 — the re-profile of the row (no change in the tree)

Method unchanged from encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02.meta so the two are
comparable: /usr/bin/sample, 1 ms, 60 s window started 5 s in;
self(node) = count - sum(children) summed over every call site; symbols
classified by name with the unmatched residual printed. The port arm was
sampled TWICE (the first run is the stability control, the second is the one
the table uses), libaom-c once.

  (cd benchmarks/xbench && nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c \
      -I<primary>/upstream -I<primary>/upstream/build \
      -L<primary>/upstream/build -laom -lm -lc++
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/tmp/intra8-dbg-target \
      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2 nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  dsymutil $HOME/tmp/intra8-dbg-target/release/drv-aom

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_control.sh 9 ~/tmp/intra8/control_base.tsv
  PORT_DBG=$HOME/tmp/intra8-dbg-target/release/drv-aom \
    nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg   600 60 ~/tmp/intra8/port.sample.txt  5
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh libaom-c    1600 60 ~/tmp/intra8/libaomc.sample.txt 5
  PORT_DBG=$HOME/tmp/intra8-dbg-target/release/drv-aom \
    nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg   600 60 ~/tmp/intra8/port2.sample.txt 5
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/intra8/port2.sample.txt   --arm port     --rules rust --tsv ~/tmp/intra8/port2.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/intra8/libaomc.sample.txt --arm libaom-c --rules c    --tsv ~/tmp/intra8/c.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_align.py --port ~/tmp/intra8/port2.tsv --port-ms 150.539 \
                                 --c    ~/tmp/intra8/c.tsv     --c-ms     45.602

The LIKE-FOR-LIKE class table in the .md (§1, §1b) is NOT the stage rollup — the
stage rules file the port's `aom_dsp::intra::cfl` under `dsp:intra-pred` and
libaom's `cfl_*` under `intra-mode-rd`, and libaom's `av1_predict_intra_block`
under `intra-mode-rd` while the port's equivalent is `dsp:intra-pred`. The class
sums in the .md were computed by symbol regex over the two `.tsv` rollups
(published as .stage.tsv) and RECONCILE to the combined stage gap:
7.58 (predictors) + 6.30 (RD drivers) + 1.52 (cfl) = 15.40 vs 15.39.

Denominators used: port 150.539 ms (median of the 600 timed reps taken DURING
port sample run 2), libaom-c 45.602 ms (median of its 1600).

## Part 2 — the call census

TEMPORARY atomic counters in `predict_intra_high` (class x tx_size x bd) and in
`dir::z1_high`/`z2_high`/`z3_high` (per-branch pixel counts), plus a throwaway
crates/aom-bench/examples/intra_census.rs. All removed before the commit —
neither is in the tree. Both are preserved for re-running at
  ~/tmp/intra8/census_instrumentation.patch     (the aom-dsp counters)
  ~/tmp/intra8/intra_census.rs                  (the example)
and the raw output is committed as .census.txt. One encode of the profile cell;
counts are exact (one relaxed atomic per call, single-threaded).

## Part 3 — the arms and the bands

Six port arms plus the C oracle, ONE binary each, all built from this one
worktree by patching the three `*_vec_applies` gate predicates and restoring
from git (~/tmp/intra8/build_arms.sh; SHA-verified distinct):

  base      0279544, no dir_simd at all
  baseB     a SECOND COPY of the identical `base` binary — the box's noise floor
            on the PRE side, measured rather than asserted (playbook §6)
  z1z3      only z1/z3 vectorized (z2's gate forced false)
  z2        only z2's above-half vectorized (z1's and z3's gates forced false)
  all       71c924a as it ships
  allB      a SECOND COPY of `all` — the noise floor on the POST side
  allC      `all` with the kernel's per-lane store loop replaced by
            bitcast_u16x16 + copy_from_slice. MEASURED WORSE (0.15 pp) and
            NOT SHIPPED; see the .md §5.
  libaom-c  benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c against the pinned oracle

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh 24 <ab_split.tsv> \
      base=... baseB=... z1z3=... z2=... all=... allB=... libaom-c=...
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh 36 <ab.tsv> \
      base=... baseB=... all=... allB=... allC=... libaom-c=...
  python3 scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py <tsv> --ref libaom-c --vs base

The 36-round band is the headline (its nulls are -0.01 % / -0.06 %); the
24-round band is BOTH an independent replication of the headline and the
per-half split, and its nulls are -0.17 % / +0.07 pp, which is why the second
band was run. Neither band is averaged into the other.

The two halves are measured separately even though they ship together, per
playbook §14's closing rule.

## Byte-identity gate

  AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR=<primary checkout>/conformance/data \
    cargo nextest run --cargo-profile test-fast -j 4 --workspace \
      --no-fail-fast --run-ignored all                     # SIMD dispatch live
  AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1 <same>                                # scalar-pinned
  cargo check --target x86_64-apple-darwin --workspace --all-targets

`--run-ignored all` is included deliberately (the KB-PERF-1 session found two
stale pins that way). This worktree borrows the primary checkout's gitignored
conformance/data READ-ONLY via AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR; without it ~40 tests
fail-loud with "conformance vector missing", which is the fail-loud design
working, not a regression.

Gate 2 (`config_permutations`, every --cpu-used 0..9 cell byte-exact against
real aomenc) was ALSO run on its own before the commit: 87 passed / 0 failed /
3 ignored.

RESULTS: SIMD-live 964/964 passed, 0 skipped, 730 s. The scalar-pinned leg
failed ONCE, on `intra::dir_simd::tests::the_tap_bound_is_load_bearing` — a
defect in the TEST, not in the code (under AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1 the dispatch entry
routes to the scalar core, so it cannot diverge from itself). Fixed by
conditioning the divergence half on `dispatch::scalar_forced()` and keeping the
gate's own 1024-rejects/1023-accepts half unconditional; nothing was relaxed.
Both modes then pass. The three benchmarks/config_perm_*_2026-07-30.tsv evidence
sweeps regenerate identical apart from the commit stamp and the `ms` timing
column (diffed column-wise: 0 non-timing differing data rows in all three), so
they are left as committed.

## x86-64 / Windows

  gh workflow run winperf.yml -f base_sha=0279544522a77a7d6be81a66b02a53a1abdabed8 \
                              -f rounds=16 -f arms=prepost

`arms: prepost` builds `pre` from a whole-src checkout of base_sha and `post`
from the dispatched ref, with `l3a` and `l3` as extra COPIES of `pre` — so each
band carries THREE nulls (two pre-side, one post-side). Two runners
(windows-11-arm, windows-latest x86-64) x two contents (`detail`, `smooth`),
never averaged. Bands committed as .win_<runner>_<content>.tsv.

Run 30792984795. RESULT: NOT RESOLVED on either runner — every post-vs-pre
figure is at or under that band's own same-binary nulls (see the .md §7).

The winperf CONTENT caveat is much stronger for this lever than it was for the
i16-forward one, and it was CHECKED rather than assumed: the same census was
re-run on winperf's `detail` and `smooth` (committed as .census_winperf.txt,
produced by the same temporary counters plus a `detail`/`smooth` mode added to
the throwaway example). Directional predicted pixels: photograph 20.8 %,
winperf `smooth` 13.2 %, winperf `detail` **0.15 %**. winperf's sources were
tuned to bracket the photograph's ALLOCATOR CALL COUNT, not its mode
distribution, so `detail` is a structurally vacuous cell for a directional-intra
lever. Any future lever scoped to a mode family must run this census before
reading a winperf band.

## Known gaps

* ONE cell, one image, one content class. cpu-used 3/4/5/9 are not measured;
  the re-profile records 9 (5.64x) and 4 (7.76x) as worse cells, neither
  decomposed. The census is likewise one cell — at a preset that picks
  different block sizes and angles the 68.4 % addressable share moves.
* 8-bit 4:2:0 only. The gate is a runtime bound so bd12 blocks decline; the
  cost of that decline is folded into the reported win rather than isolated
  (no arm was built with the scan present and the vector path disabled).
* Wall clock only. No instruction count (no valgrind on Apple Silicon).
* Single-threaded, one frame.
* The rest of the intra predictor class (+4.45 ms: smooth/paeth, the edge
  filter, the DC/V/H fills) is untouched and nothing here measures what it
  would return.
* Linux is unmeasured.
* x86-64 is compiled (`cargo check --target x86_64-apple-darwin`) and
  differentially tested (the v3/AVX2 tier is in the 25 token permutations
  `dir_simd_diff` runs), but NOT usefully timed anywhere — see §7.

## Committed data files

  .ab.tsv         the 36-round headline band, 6 arms x 36 rounds
  .ab_split.tsv   the 24-round band, 7 arms, per-half split + replication
  .control.tsv    the session-opening 2-arm control (9 interleaved invocations)
  .stage.tsv      the like-for-like intra symbol table, both arms, self ms
  .census.txt     the intra call census (raw tool output)
  .win_<runner>_<content>.tsv   the Windows bands
